TryHackMe (free room)


1. Overview
A hands-on introduction to the Windows desktop. The room uses a story: it is the first day at a fictional company called TryHatMe, and the learner works on a Windows Server 2019 workstation. It covers the interface, File Explorer, system information, installing and removing software, settings, Task Manager and the built-in security tools.
2. Learning objectives
Move around the desktop, taskbar and Start menu.
Use File Explorer and understand file paths.
Check system details and change settings.
Use Task Manager, Windows Security and the firewall to monitor and protect the system.
3. Task-by-task notes
Task 1: Introduction
Started the lab machine in split-screen. The room expects earlier knowledge of computer parts, computer types and what an operating system does.
Task 2: Exploring the Windows workspace
Authentication: before the desktop appears, a user proves identity with a password, PIN or other method. Account types: Guest (very limited), Standard (everyday tasks) and Administrator (full control). The lab logs in as Administrator.
Desktop parts: desktop icons, Start menu, search, Task View, pinned apps, network and audio icons, date and time, notifications.
Built-in tools: Notepad, File Explorer, Settings.
Your PC shows the device name, RAM and Windows version.
File Explorer: Windows uses a folder hierarchy. The address bar shows the full path, here C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\TryHatMe Onboarding.

Task 3: Configuring and securing Windows
Updates: Windows Update patches the OS. Apps update in different ways (automatic, prompts, or manual installers).
Installing: from the Microsoft Store (not on Windows Server by default) or from a vendor's .exe or .msi installer. Uninstalling: Store, Add or remove programs, Control Panel, or the app's own uninstaller.
Settings vs Control Panel: Settings is the modern location, and Control Panel is the older tool still used for some admin tasks.
Task Manager tabs: Processes, Performance, Users, Details (shows PIDs) and Services.
Windows Security: Virus & threat protection, Firewall & network protection, App & browser control, Device security. I ran a custom scan on the onboarding folder, which detected the harmless EICAR test file.
Windows Defender Firewall profiles: Domain, Private and Public. Advanced settings show inbound and outbound rules.
Task 4: Conclusion
Key terms recapped: Desktop, Taskbar, Start menu, Search, File Explorer, Windows Update, Microsoft Store, Settings, Control Panel, Task Manager, Windows Security and Windows Defender Firewall.
4. Key takeaways
Know the default tools, because attackers and defenders both use them.
Task Manager and the Details tab help spot unusual processes.
The firewall and antivirus are built in, so check they are on.

